 "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/54314"} "34 from 21 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "10 metres platform."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Although the Chinese women dominated the sport by 2004, they were not quite as good on platform as on springboard. The 2003 World Champion was Canada’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/90552"} "Émilie Heymans"] ", who was born in Belgium but moved to Canada with her family when she was only a 1-year-old. The 2004 World Cup went to the defending Olympic champion, American " [:a {:href "/athletes/90639"} "Laura Wilkinson"] ". In the end the gold in this event would go to the surprising Australian " [:a {:href "/athletes/52072"} "Chantelle Newbery"] ", who was only sixth after qualifying and third after the semi-final round. She had never won a major platform title before and had been away from the sport after the 2000 Olympics while giving birth to a son. Newbery was married to " [:a {:href "/athletes/52113"} "Robert Newbery"] ", who won three synchro bronze medals in 2000-04."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Silver went to " [:a {:href "/athletes/107920"} "Lao Lishi"] ", who had been runner-up at 2003 Worlds and led after the semis. Her teammate " [:a {:href ""} "Li Ting"] " was second after the semi-finals but dropped out of the medals in the final round, placing sixth. Heymans also failed to medal, after a very poor semi-final performance. The bronze went to " [:a {:href "/athletes/45134"} "Loudy Tourky"] ", an Australian of Palestine heritage, who had been second at the 2004 World Cup."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/52072"} "Chantelle Newbery"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle",